theorem average-above-left
  note        The average (A + B)/2 lies above A exactly when B does: the
              average minus A is half of B − A.
  let A ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let B ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  then A < B ↔ A < (A + B)/2

1.  A < B ↔ A < (A + B)/2
    both directions

    direction
    assume A < B                                                      (D1)

    1.1.  A < (A + B)/2
          inequalities
            A < B                D1
            A + A < A + B        adding A
            2·A < A + B          algebra
            A < (A + B)/2        multiplying by 1/2

    direction
    assume A < (A + B)/2                                              (D2)

    1.2.  A < B
          inequalities
            A < (A + B)/2        D2
            2·A < A + B          multiplying by 2
            A < B                adding −A
